@node Running Jacal, Canonical, Introduction, Introduction
@section Running Jacal
@cindex Running Jacal

If you successfully executed one of the installations of the previous
section, then typing @samp{jacal} or clicking an icon will begin an
interactive session.

To manually start jacal, start your Scheme implementation with SLIB.
This may involve setting up that implementation's initialization file or
LOADing a @samp{.init} file from the @file{slib} directory.  Then type:

@example
(slib:load "/usr/local/lib/jacal/math")
@end example

@noindent
where @file{/usr/local/lib/jacal/} is a path to the JACAL directory.
JACAL should then print:

@example
JACAL version @value{JACALVERSION}, Copyright 1989-2026 Aubrey Jaffer
JACAL comes with ABSOLUTELY NO WARRANTY; for details type `(terms)'.
This is free software, and you are welcome to redistribute it
under certain conditions; type `(terms)' for details.
;;; Type (math); to begin from Scheme session.
@end example

@noindent
Do as it says:

@example
(math)
@result{}
type qed; to return to scheme, type help; for help.
(%0000)
@end example

@noindent
And you are ready to try the commands described in the rest of the
manual.

@subheading Recovery from Errors
@cindex Recovery from Errors

JACAL tries to catch any errors it encounters and print an informative
message before returning to the prompt.  If there are unmatched
parentheses or missing delimiters in your typed command, type the
closing delimiters, digits for ``expression missing'', and semicolon
(followed by newline) until it returns to the prompt:

@example
@group
(%0000) (@{adf;
          ^ "mismatched delimiter" #\; expecting #\@} 
;
^ "mismatched delimiter" #\; expecting #\) 
;
%0000: adf
(%0001) 
@end group
@end example

If that is unsuccessful, typing @samp{CTRL-C} will likely return you
to the underlying Scheme session.

As JACAL is a complicated program there are bugs which will occasionally
cause the program to stop with some sort of error reported by the
underlying Scheme system.  In interactive implementations (such as SCM)
you can usually continue your session by typing @code{(math)}.  The
expression which was input to JACAL just before the error will be lost
but you should be able to otherwise continue with your session.

If you define identifiers which have already been used in formulas,
the earlier uses are not updated.  You can restore a name to its
unassigned status by defining it to itself:

@example
@group
(%0001) a: 5/7;
a: 5/7
(%0001) a+3;
       26
%0001: --
       7
(%0002) a:a;
a: a
(%0002) a+3;
%0002: 3 + a
@end group
@end example

If the underlying scheme implementation supports a @code{(restart)}
command, then typing @code{restart();} in JACAL will re-initiate the
session, forgetting all the commands and formulas you entered
previously:

@example
@group
(%0003) restart();
..............
JACAL version 2a1, Copyright 1989-2026 Aubrey Jaffer
JACAL comes with ABSOLUTELY NO WARRANTY; for details type `(terms)'.
This is free software, and you are welcome to redistribute it
under certain conditions; type `(terms)' for details.
;;; Type (math); to begin from Scheme session.
Type qed; to return to Scheme, type help; for help.
(%0000)
@end group
@end example

@subheading Stopping Jacal
@cindex Stopping Jacal

The command @code{quit();} will end your JACAL session.

With non-interactive Scheme implementations the JACAL command
@code{qed;} or typing the end-of-file character (@key{C-z} on MS-DOS
and VMS, @key{C-d} on others) will end your JACAL session.

The command @code{qed;} will return to the interactive Scheme session.
Typing @code{(math)} will return to the JACAL session.

From the interactive Scheme session @code{(exit)} or possibly an
end-of-file character will terminate the session.


@node Canonical, Release Notes, Running Jacal, Introduction
@section Canonical
@cindex Canonical

The canonical aspect of JACAL is that all equations and expressions
involving variables undergo nomalization such that two formulas which
are equivalent have the same representation.  This representation is
affected by variable ordering; substituting one variable name for
another looks different but expresses the same mathematical
constraint.

As with all symbolic algebra systems, roots of unity (1) are not
uniquely normalizable.  JACAL's guarantees apply only to expressions
involving variables.

JACAL prioritizes simplicity of expressions over full canonical
reductions.  For instance, all trigonometric functions can be
expressed as (complex number) exponentials and logarithms; but it is
difficult to understand that representation.  Instead, trigonometric
expressions are represented in terms of tangent (tan) and arctangent
(atan).

A transcendental function composed with its inverse cancels each other
if the argument to the inverse function involves at least one
variable.  @code{exp(@var{integer}*log(@var{argument}))} reduces to
@code{@var{argument}^(@var{integer})} if @var{argument} involves at
least one variable.  @code{exp(log(@var{argument})/@var{integer})}
does not reduce if @var{integer} is greater than one.  The @code{^}
function reduces this case to a radical:

@example
@group
(%0000) %expt(a+x,1/5);
           log(a + x)
%0000: exp(----------)
               5
(%0001) (a+x)^(1/5);
              1/5
%0001: (a + x)
@end group
@end example

Trignometric functions have similar reductions:

@example
@group
(%0002) sin(asin(x));
%0002: x
(%0003) asin(sin(x));
%0003: x
(%0004) sin(3*asin(x));
                  3
%0004: - 3 x + 4 x
(%0005) tan(3*atan(x));
                3
       - 3 x + x
%0005: ----------
               2
       -1 + 3 x
(%0006) atan(3*tan(x));
%0006: atan(3 tan(x))
(%0007) tan(6*atan(x));
                   3      5
         6 x - 20 x  + 6 x
%0007: ----------------------
               2       4    6
       1 - 15 x  + 15 x  - x
@end group
@end example

The Lambert-W function (@code{%W}) is the inverse of
@code{@var{z}*exp(@var{z})}.  Currently the reduction works in one
direction; the other will eventually be supported:

@example
@group
(%0008) %W(x*exp(x));
%0008: x
(%0009) %W(x)*exp(%W(x));
%0009: %W(x) exp(%W(x))
@end group
@end example

Because transcendental functions are defined by their differential
equations, JACAL can produce their derivatives:

@example
@group
(%0015) diff(%W(x),x);
          %W(x)
%0015: -----------
       x + x %W(x)
(%0016) diff(tan(x),x);
                   2
%0016: 1 + (tan(x))
(%0017) diff(sin(x),x);
               1
%0017: ------------------
                    2 1/2
       (1 + (tan(x)) )
(%0018) diff(cos(x),x);
              tan(x)
%0018: --------------------
                      2 1/2
       - (1 + (tan(x)) )
(%0019) diff(%expt(x,1/5),x);
           log(x)
       exp(------)
             5
%0019: -----------
           5 x
(%0020) diff(x^(1/5),x);
         1
%0020: ------
          4/5
       5 x
@end group
@end example

The usual algebraic reductions also work:
@example
(%0024) sin(x/a)^2+cos(x/a)^2;
%0024: 1
@end example

JACAL provides some functions which it does not canonicalize:
@code{imagpart}, @code{realpart}, @code{abs}, and @code{cabs}.


@node Release Notes, Conventions, Canonical, Introduction
@section Release Notes
@cindex Release Notes

JACAL-2a1 is a major release incorporating transcendental functions.

Do not raise transcendental functions to powers like @code{sin^2(x)}.
Instead, use @code{sin(x)^2}.

Trigonometric sum and difference of angle formulas do not reduce.

@node Conventions,  , Release Notes, Introduction
@section Conventions
@cindex Conventions

Things that are labeled as Operators can occur in expressions output by
Jacal.  Things that are labeled as Commands act upon their arguments and
do not generally occur in expressions output by Jacal.  Things that are
labeled as flags are @code{set} to control aspects of the Jacal
environment.

The examples throughout this text were produced using the SCM Scheme
implementation.

Jacal has several input grammars it understands.  The @code{standard}
input grammar (which is the same as the @code{std} and @code{disp2d}
input grammars) is used in this manual.  It is very similar to
@code{Maxima} input grammar and the Algol family of computer
languages.

Identifier names are case sensitive.


